\title{Munsee Language Resources for K-12 Curriculum Development}
\author{Compiled by Claude Sonnet 5 (claude-sonnet-5) for Andr\'as Kornai}
\date{}
\begin{document}
\maketitle
\section{Introduction}
Munsee (Munsee Delaware; ISO 639-3 umu) is an Eastern Algonquian language, critically endangered. Of the Canadian sites where it was predominant, only Moraviantown, Ontario has surviving speakers. This search found one major dictionary and a community digital dictionary project, but no ready-made school curriculum.
\section{Primary Educational Resources}
No textbooks or primers were found in search results.
\section{Linguistic and Reference Materials}
\begin{itemize}
\item John O'Meara, \emph{Delaware-English / English-Delaware Dictionary} (University of Toronto Press, 1996; paperback 2014). About 7,100 Delaware-English entries based on Moraviantown speakers, with sample sentences and usage notes. \url{https://www.hfsbooks.com/books/delaware-english-english-delaware-dictionary-omeara}
\item Language Museum listing of the same dictionary. \url{https://languagemuseum.ca/books/delaware-english-english-delaware-dictionary/}
\end{itemize}
\section{Religious and Community Resources}
No religious texts were found. The Munsee-Delaware Nation community runs language activities (see below).
\section{Digital and Audio Resources}
\begin{itemize}
\item Princeton Linguistics news item on the ``Towards a Digital Munsee Dictionary'' prototype, which grew out of the Lunaape Language Camp of July 2023, presented at the Munsee Language Symposium. \url{https://linguistics.princeton.edu/2023/12/08/junior-sreeniketh-vogoti-presents-ongoing-linguistics-project-at-munsee-language-symposium/}
\end{itemize}
\section{Curriculum Development Context}
With about nine speakers, a curriculum would depend on the O'Meara dictionary and close work with the Munsee-Delaware Nation and its language teachers. No school programme was found.
\end{document}
